Description: Implements quantile-on-quantile regression (QQR) as introduced
    by Sim and Zhou (2015) <doi:10.1016/j.jbankfin.2015.02.030> for
    exploring how the effect of a covariate varies jointly across the
    conditional quantiles of a response and the quantiles of a designated
    regressor. The main fitting function 'qqr()' uses local linear quantile
    regression with Gaussian kernel weights to estimate coefficient and
    p-value surfaces over user-specified grids of outcome and regressor
    quantiles. Eight automatic bandwidth selection methods are provided to
    promote reproducibility and remove researcher discretion over this
    critical tuning parameter: Silverman's (1986) rule of thumb, a robust
    IQR-corrected rule (Scott 1992), an IQR-scaled rule, a fast
    leave-one-out cross-validation selector (Yu and Jones 1998, renamed
    'cv_fast'), the Sheather-Jones solve-the-equation plug-in
    (Sheather and Jones 1991, 'sj'), the Ruppert-Sheather-Wand direct
    plug-in for local linear regression (Ruppert, Sheather and Wand 1995,
    'yj'), np full least-squares cross-validation via 'npregbw()'
    (Hayfield and Racine 2008, 'npcv'), and np smooth-coefficient
    cross-validation via 'npscoefbw()' (Li and Racine 2010, 'sc').
    The selected bandwidth, formula, numerical inputs and bibliographic
    reference are stored in the fitted object and printed automatically.
    Additional methods support printing and summarising QQR objects with
    full bandwidth diagnostics, producing static and interactive
    three-dimensional coefficient surfaces ('surface3d()',
    'panel_surface()', 'plot.qqr()', 'Int.plot()'), extracting surfaces
    as tidy tables ('qqr_surface()', 'rowave.qqr()'), exporting results
    with significance stars to Excel and Word ('excel.qqr()',
    'word.qqr()'), and comparing row-averaged QQR effects with standard
    quantile regression coefficients ('compare.qqr()',
    'plot.compare.qqr()', 'excel.compare.qqr()', 'word.compare.qqr()').
    Core quantile regression algorithms use 'quantreg'
    (Koenker 2005 <doi:10.1017/CBO9780511754098>). The 'sj' and
    'yj' selectors require only base R or the recommended 'KernSmooth'
    package; the 'npcv' and 'sc' selectors require the 'np' package.
